1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,412
An elevator is closed.

2
00:00:01,412 --> 00:00:03,273
The workaround uses two more.

3
00:00:03,273 --> 00:00:05,520
What if one of those is closed too?

4
00:00:06,170 --> 00:00:08,502
LiftCheck checks the mapped station path.

5
00:00:08,502 --> 00:00:13,848
In this labeled replay, closing Assembly's Foley Street elevator
reveals a different entrance.

6
00:00:13,848 --> 00:00:17,090
Its two elevators are checked as part of the alternative.

7
00:00:17,990 --> 00:00:21,535
Close the second one, and the alternative disappears.

8
00:00:21,535 --> 00:00:24,946
Expire the reports, and the answer becomes unknown.

9
00:00:24,946 --> 00:00:26,350
No stale green light.

10
00:00:27,250 --> 00:00:29,941
Now switch to current M B T A reports.

11
00:00:29,941 --> 00:00:33,341
Pick the entrance and platform you actually use.

12
00:00:33,341 --> 00:00:38,370
Results show mapped facilities, source times, and any missing
evidence.

13
00:00:39,270 --> 00:00:41,123
Save those choices as a journey.

14
00:00:41,123 --> 00:00:42,976
Reload, and they're still there.

15
00:00:42,976 --> 00:00:49,230
Up to four station checks share one alert snapshot, instead of
mixing evidence collected at different times.

16
00:00:50,131 --> 00:00:53,495
These are real calls to our self-hosted M C P server.

17
00:00:53,495 --> 00:00:58,891
The browser and the assistant-facing tools use the same path
logic and saved context.

18
00:00:59,790 --> 00:01:04,214
The prototype covers three stations and checks published
reports.

19
00:01:04,214 --> 00:01:06,663
It does not certify physical access.

20
00:01:06,663 --> 00:01:10,270
Alexa device use and rider testing remain unverified.

21
00:01:11,171 --> 00:01:11,845
LiftCheck.

22
00:01:11,845 --> 00:01:12,856
Check the path.

23
00:01:12,856 --> 00:01:14,407
Recheck the workaround.

24
00:01:14,407 --> 00:01:17,171
Source and reproducible tests are linked.
